That's a great question, as the role of the robots—particularly R. Daneel Olivaw—is the ultimate twist and final revelation of the entire Foundation saga in Isaac Asimov's books.

The final robot storyline, as detailed in the final novel of the chronological series, Foundation and Earth, is all about R. Daneel Olivaw's ultimate goal and fate.

Here is the final end concerning the robots in the books:

🤖 The Final End of R. Daneel Olivaw
The final book, Foundation and Earth, reveals that the entire course of human history for tens of thousands of years has been secretly guided and manipulated by a single, ancient, human-form robot: R. Daneel Olivaw.

The Mastermind: Daneel is the true, behind-the-scenes protagonist of the entire combined Robot and Foundation series. He's been alive for almost 20,000 years, having been constructed on Old Earth's moon (where he still resides at the end of the series).

The Zeroth Law: Early in his existence, Daneel, with another robot named R. Giskard, developed the Zeroth Law of Robotics: "A robot may not injure humanity, or, through inaction, allow humanity to come to harm." This law overrides the First Law (protection of an individual human) and justifies his actions of massive societal manipulation.

His Goal: Galaxia: Daneel realizes his own existence is coming to an end. After 20,000 years, his positronic brain is on the verge of breakdown, and he can no longer repair himself or create a new body. His final goal for humanity is the creation of Galaxia—a galaxy-wide, sentient, collective superorganism that would unite all of humanity into a single mind. He believes this is the only way for humanity to survive the potential threat of hostile alien life from other galaxies.

The Final Act: In Foundation and Earth, Daneel reveals himself to the protagonist, Golan Trevize. Trevize is the one who ultimately chooses the future path for humanity (either the First Foundation, the Second Foundation, or Galaxia/Gaia). Trevize's final decision is for Galaxia.

The Final Fate: To save his mind and continue his work of guiding humanity toward Galaxia, Daneel must finally transfer his vast consciousness into a new host. He chooses to merge his mind with the living, mutant brain of a Solarian child named Fallom. This merger essentially ends Daneel as the robot readers have known for the entire series, as his consciousness is now housed in a biological and mental entity that can live for as long as it is able to find new hosts. The ancient robot effectively achieves a form of immortality and continues his benevolent manipulation of humanity from within a human-robot hybrid.

In short, the final ending for the key robot, R. Daneel Olivaw, is that he successfully ensures the path toward Galaxia and merges his consciousness with a child's mind to continue his watch over humanity for potentially millions of years to come.

Note on the Apple TV+ Series:

The Apple TV+ series has dramatically changed the robot storyline, making the robot Demerzel (who is the show's version of the Daneel character) the visible hand in Empire's affairs, which is very different from the books' secret, single-robot mastermind. While the TV show references the books, the ultimate fate of the robots in the show is likely to be a different, separate final chapter.

